he primary PFS analysis (6-monthly survival status contact) via protocol amendment, with SAP-specified OS analyses at the Q4 2027 interim and at maturity (target ~60% of ITT events or 48 months after last patient randomised), including RPSFT/IPCW switching adjustment and RMST, plus a documented follow-up plan with maturity triggers","gap_coverage":"Adds the randomised, switching-adjusted OS evidence and a maturity-triggered follow-up plan. The parent TAC-015 only names long-term OS follow-up and has no analysis specification.","cost_effort":"Low to moderate incremental cost: survival-status contacts, subsequent-therapy data capture, and statistical work on the SAP. No new sites or enrolment.","timing":"Interim OS in Q4 2027, with the maturity cut about 2030. Neither is available for the 2027-03-31 decision, so the submissions would cite the follow-up plan and pre-specified methods.","feasibility_risks":"The amendment must be approved by sponsor governance, regulators and ethics committees, and none of this is assumed approved. Risks: loss to follow-up after the primary analysis, heavy censoring, switching-adjustment assumptions that HTA bodies may challenge, and incomplete subsequent-therapy data.","post_hoc":false,"prospective_enrolment":false,"protocol_amendment":true,"start_date":null,"evidence_available":null},"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: randomised OS with switching adjustment is the highest-quality evidence for this gap, ahead of registry-based OS (confounded, no randomised comparator) or a standalone new study. Cost and effort: expanding TAC-015 is cheaper than a new study because it uses the existing VEL-301 infrastructure and adds only follow-up contacts and analysis work. Time: the Q4 2027 interim is the earliest randomised OS, but the maturity cut is slow. Feasibility: it depends on an amendment to an ongoing trial, which is routine but unapproved. A new standalone OS study was rejected because it would duplicate TAC-001 and TAC-015. Expanding TAC-001 directly would blur the follow-up scope that TAC-015 already owns.","population":"Randomised VEL-301 ITT patients with second-line EGFR-mutant NSCLC after osimertinib, including patients aged 65 and over and those with baseline brain metastases as supportive subgroups","comparator":"Osimertinib re-challenge or investigator's choice (VEL-301 control arm)","outcomes":"Overall survival (HR, KM medians and landmark rates at 12/24/36/48 months), RMST difference at pre-specified truncation times, crossover/subsequent-therapy-adjusted OS HR (RPSFT and IPCW), proportion switching or receiving subsequent therapy","data_source":"VEL-301 trial database with extended survival-status follow-up and subsequent-therapy data capture","study_design":"Prospective extended survival follow-up of all randomised VEL-301 patients after the primary PFS analysis (6-monthly survival status contact) via protocol amendment, with SAP-specified OS analyses at the Q4 2027 interim and at maturity (target ~60% of ITT events or 48 months after last patient randomised), including RPSFT/IPCW switching adjustment and RMST, plus a documented follow-up plan with maturity triggers","duration_months":48,"readout_lag_months":4,"timing_rationale":"Duration runs from the amendment through to the maturity data cut about 2030. This is event-driven and follows the Q4 2027 interim. The lag covers database lock, switching-adjusted modelling and SAP-specified sensitivity analyses. The interim readout comes earlier in Q4 2027.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""},
