sent and legal-basis procedures at enrolment, scheduled linkage to national death registries, pre-specified descriptive OS analyses at annual cuts and at 36 months, and a vital-status completeness target of 90% or more","gap_coverage":"Adds verified, long-horizon OS in routine care. The parent registry covers treatment patterns, effectiveness, safety and PROs but does not specify verified OS or linkage.","cost_effort":"Moderate incremental cost: linkage fees, data-protection assessments in each country, and consent changes. No new sites or enrolment beyond the registry.","timing":"Registry enrolment starts around launch. An early descriptive OS cut would be about 12-18 months after launch and the 36-month cut about 2030, so none of it is available before the 2027-03-31 decision.","feasibility_risks":"Linkage requirements vary by country, and some EU5 markets need long approval timelines. Mortality-register access lags, immortal-time and selection biases are possible, and there is no randomised comparator. Registry enrolment and protocol approval are not yet secured, so all of this depends on the registry protocol being finalised and amended.","post_hoc":false,"prospective_enrolment":false,"protocol_amendment":true,"start_date":null,"evidence_available":null},"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: lower than randomised OS (no randomised comparator, selection and linkage bias) but it reflects routine care and adds verified vital status, which standalone registry follow-up lacks. Cost and effort: moderate, and cheaper than a new mortality-linked cohort because it reuses the planned registry. Time: slower to a meaningful read-out because enrolment must accrue first. Feasibility: linkage approvals vary by country. A new standalone claims-based OS study was rejected because it would duplicate TAC-008 and TAC-021 and lack clinical and mutation detail. It is a complement to proposal 1 rather than a substitute, with proposal 1 as the primary mature OS evidence.","populat