ts; non-frail registry patients serve as the internal reference.","comparator":"Non-frail Velmara patients in the same registry (internal reference). No external comparator.","outcomes":"EQ-5D-5L utility and VAS; EORTC QLQ-C30 and QLQ-LC13 scores; time to deterioration in global health status and physical function; 12-month PRO completion rates; caregiver burden (Zarit-12) in a subset.","data_source":"Prospective Velmara registry, with ePRO and clinician-reported frailty data.","study_design":"Frailty-targeted oversampling stratum embedded in the prospective multinational Velmara registry. Frailty is screened at enrolment. PROs are collected at baseline and every 3 months to 12 months, with a prespecified SAP and a PRO completion-support protocol.","duration_months":24,"readout_lag_months":4,"timing_rationale":"Assumes about 12 months to recruit about 150 frail patients, plus 12 months of follow-up, giving 24 months to last data. Readout takes about 4 months for PRO data cleaning, SAP-driven analysis and report. Registry start timing is not yet confirmed.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""},
{"id":"GAP-027:proposal:2","gap_id":"GAP-027","name":"Standalone prospective frail-patient PRO cohort on Velmara","type":"pro_study","evidence_question":"What is the change in HTA-grade health-related quality of life over 6 months in frail patients with EGFR-mutant NSCLC treated with Velmara, relative to non-frail patients treated with Velmara?","rationale":"A dedicated PRO study can start at launch, independent of registry timing. It is built around frail patients and HTA-accepted instruments, and can support EU5 HTA resubmissions and US P&T review of frail-patient value.","proposal_kind":"new","target_tactic_id":null,"expansion_scope":null,"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: Velmara-specific, prospective PRO data in frail patients with a non-frail reference, essentially the same quality as the registry stratum. Dedicated recruitment gives better frailty phenotyping and PRO compliance support. Cost and effort: higher than the registry expansion, because it needs new sites, a protocol, ethics approvals and a data platform. Time: can start at launch without waiting for registry start, with a shorter 6-month follow-up. Feasibility: needs a new study approval and site contracts. It must not duplicate the registry, so it should be dropped if the registry stratum is approved. It is distinct from the non-counting TAC-013 survey, which is a cross-sectional experience survey, because it uses longitudinal, Velmara-specific HTA instruments. I considered expanding TAC-013, but it is only proposed, not active, and cannot be made Velmara-specific.","population":"Adults with EGFR-mutant NSCLC starting Velmara who are frail (G8 score ≤14 and/or ECOG PS 2), with a non-frail reference group at a 1:1 ratio. Target about 120 frail patients across US and EU5 oncology centres, including community sites.","comparator":"Non-frail Velmara patients enrolled concurrently (internal reference).","outcomes":"Primary: change in EORTC QLQ-C30 global health status at 6 months. Secondary: EQ-5D-5L utilities for cost-effectiveness inputs, QLQ-LC13 symptom scales, time to deterioration, treatment discontinuation, and caregiver burden (Zarit-12).","data_source":"Prospective primary data collection by ePRO and clinic assessment, with a G8 frailty screen and medical record abstraction for clinical covariates.","study_design":"Prospective, multicentre, observational PRO cohort. Baseline and monthly assessments for 3 months, then every 3 months to 6 months. Mixed-model repeated measures SAP aligned to HTA guidance. Missing data handled by pattern-mixture or multiple-imputation sensitivity analyses. A preagreed protocol is shared with payer advisers.","duration_months":18,"readout_lag_months":4,"timing_rationale":"About 12 months to enrol about 120 frail patients and 6 months of follow-up for the last patient, giving 18 months. Readout lag of about 4 months for database lock, PRO analysis and report. Site and ethics set-up before the first patient is not included.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""},
{"id":"GAP-029:proposal:1","gap_id":"GAP-029","name":"Post-osimertinib NX-441 and platinum chemotherapy comparator cohorts in the sequencing study","type":"rwe_study","evidence_question":"What treatment pathways and outcomes follow osimertinib discontinuation in patients who receive NX-441 or platinum chemotherapy, and how does Velmara's position compare with these sequences?","rationale":"TAC-009 is the intended closer. Specifying NX-441 and platinum chemotherapy as named, prespecified comparator cohorts, with defined outcomes and adjustment, puts the comparator sequences into the study design so it can count toward the gap.","proposal_kind":"expansion","target_tactic_id":"TAC-009","expansion_scope":{"name":"Post-osimertinib NX-441 and platinum chemotherapy comparator cohorts in the sequencing study","evidence_question":"What treatment pathways and outcomes follow osimertinib discontinuation in patients who receive NX-441 or platinum chemotherapy, and how does Velmara's position compare with these sequences?","population":"Adults with EGFR-mutant advanced NSCLC who discontinued osimertinib and started NX-441 or platinum-based chemotherapy as the next line, plus Velmara-treated patients where available as the reference cohort.","outcomes":"Next-line treatment distribution and pathway maps; time to next treatment or death; real-world progression-free survival; real-world overall survival; treatment discontinuation; CNS progression events; and, in elderly patients (age ≥65), time to next treatment.","geography":"US + EU5","data_cut":"Primary cut at least 12 months after the index date of each cohort. Interim descriptive pathway cut for P&T and HTA use when data allow.","analysis":"New-user cohorts indexed at the start of the next line after osimertinib. Descriptive pathway mapping. Comparative analyses with propensity or overlap weighting, adjusted for ECOG, brain metastases, age and prior therapy. Kaplan-Meier and weighted Cox models, with negative-control outcomes and E-value sensitivity analyses.","instrument":"","study_design":"Retrospective-prospective new-user cohort study in linked EHR and claims data. Index date is the start of the first line after osimertinib. Cohorts are NX-441, platinum chemotherapy and Velmara (when numbers allow), with a prespecified protocol and SAP.","gap_coverage":"Closes the comparator-pathway part of the gap by capturing NX-441 and platinum chemotherapy sequences and outcomes alongside Velmara.","cost_effort":"Moderate incremental cost over the base TAC-009: cohort definitions, data licences for NX-441 and chemotherapy patients, and weighting analyses. No new patient enrolment.","timing":"Starts as soon as data access and protocol are agreed. Comparator cohorts can be read out before Velmara numbers accumulate.","feasibility_risks":"NX-441 uptake may be too small in early data. Velmara numbers will be small at first. Residual confounding from channeling bias. Variable EU5 data access.","post_hoc":false,"prospective_enrolment":false,"protocol_amendment":false,"start_date":null,"evidence_available":null},"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: comparator cohorts in the same data and design as Velmara allow weighted comparison and consistent pathway definitions, which is stronger than standalone cohorts from different sources. Cost and effort: lowest of the two options because it shares the TAC-009 protocol and data infrastructure. Time: no new study set-up, but dependent on TAC-009 being approved and counted. Feasibility: depends on NX-441 uptake and on EU5 data access. I considered a standalone comparator-only study (proposal 2), which is faster but not directly comparable to Velmara.","population":"Adults with EGFR-mutant advanced NSCLC who discontinued osimertinib and started NX-441 or platinum-based chemotherapy as the next line, plus Velmara-treated patients where available as the reference cohort.","comparator":"NX-441 versus platinum chemotherapy, each compared with Velmara where numbers allow.","outcomes":"Next-line treatment distribution and pathway maps; time to next treatment or death; real-world progression-free survival; real-world overall survival; treatment discontinuation; CNS progression events; and, in elderly patients (age ≥65), time to next treatment.","data_source":"US EHR-derived oncology database linked to claims; EU5 hospital EHR or national cancer registry data via the TAC-009 data partners.","study_design":"Retrospective-prospective new-user cohort study in linked EHR and claims data. Index date is the start of the first line after osimertinib. Cohorts are NX-441, platinum chemotherapy and Velmara (when numbers allow), with a prespecified protocol and SAP.","duration_months":18,"readout_lag_months":4,"timing_rationale":"About 18 months of data accrual and follow-up after the Velmara and NX-441 launches. Readout lag of about 4 months for data cleaning, weighting analyses and report.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""},
{"id":"GAP-029:proposal:2","gap_id":"GAP-029","name":"Comparator-only benchmark cohort study of NX-441 and platinum chemotherapy after osimertinib","type":"rwe_study","evidence_question":"What are the treatment pathways, time to next treatment and survival of patients who receive NX-441 or platinum chemotherapy after osimertinib in routine EU5 and US practice, as a benchmark for Velmara?","rationale":"Velmara real-world numbers will be small at the time of the decision. A comparator-only benchmark can use existing data now, and gives payers a reference for the NX-441 and chemotherapy sequences against which Velmara's position can later be placed.","proposal_kind":"new","target_tactic_id":null,"expansion_scope":null,"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: real-world comparator outcomes are available sooner and in larger numbers, but there is no direct Velmara comparison, so it is weaker for comparative claims than the TAC-009 expansion. Cost and effort: moderate, a separate protocol and data licences, with some overlap with TAC-009 and the natural-history study TAC-020. The design differs in using only non-Velmara cohorts, in a prespecified benchmark format. Time: faster, with no wait for Velmara uptake. Feasibility: high for US data, but NX-441 numbers depend on its launch timing. It could be merged into TAC-009 later. I considered expanding TAC-020, but its question is the natural history of recurrence, not post-osimertinib line comparators.","population":"Adults with EGFR-mutant advanced NSCLC who progressed on or discontinued osimertinib and started NX-441 or platinum-based chemotherapy as the next line, in the US and EU5. Subgroups: age ≥65 and baseline brain metastases.","comparator":"NX-441 versus platinum chemotherapy (descriptive and adjusted). No Velmara arm.","outcomes":"Pathway maps by line; time to next treatment or death; real-world progression-free survival; real-world overall survival; treatment discontinuation and reasons; CNS progression; baseline characteristics of patients receiving each sequence.","data_source":"US EHR-derived oncology database and EU5 hospital EHR or national cancer registry data. Existing data, with no new enrolment.","study_design":"Retrospective new-user cohort study with a prespecified protocol and SAP. Descriptive pathway mapping and weighted comparison between NX-441 and platinum chemotherapy cohorts. Results are formatted as a benchmark table for later comparison with Velmara data.","duration_months":9,"readout_lag_months":3,"timing_rationale":"Uses existing data, so about 9 months for protocol, data access and extraction, and cohort follow-up. Readout lag of about 3 months for analysis and reporting. EU5 data access agreements could extend this.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""}
