{"tactics":[
{"id":"GAP-021:proposal:1","gap_id":"GAP-021","name":"US claims persistence study with staged read-outs, matched comparator cohorts and age/site-type strata","type":"rwe_study","evidence_question":"What are six-month persistence and discontinuation rates for velmaratinib in US routine care compared with matched post-osimertinib comparator cohorts, and how do they differ by age group and site type, with staged read-outs for P&T?","rationale":"Payers distrust trial persistence. A claims-based new-user cohort with a matched comparator benchmark and early staged read-outs gives P&T a defensible answer in the form they asked for. The comparator benchmark can be built from historical claims before Velmara has any fills.","proposal_kind":"expansion","target_tactic_id":"TAC-008","expansion_scope":{"name":"US claims persistence study with staged read-outs, matched comparator cohorts and age/site-type strata","evidence_question":"What are six-month persistence and discontinuation rates for velmaratinib in US routine care compared with matched post-osimertinib comparator cohorts, and how do they differ by age group and site type, with staged read-outs for P&T?","population":"Adults with advanced EGFR-mutant NSCLC, identified by a claims algorithm (NSCLC diagnosis plus prior EGFR TKI exposure), with a first velmaratinib fill after US launch and at least 12 months of prior continuous enrolment. Comparators are propensity-matched patients starting a second-line regimen after osimertinib (osimertinib re-challenge, NX-441 once available, or platinum-based chemotherapy).","outcomes":"Primary: proportion persistent at 6 months (45-day permissible gap). Secondary: persistence at 3 months, time to discontinuation, proportion of days covered, switching, and dose-modification proxies. All outcomes reported by age stratum (<65, 65-74, 75+) and site type (academic vs community).","geography":"US","data_cut":"Staged cuts. Stage 0: historical comparator-only benchmark. Stage 1: about 3 months after first velmaratinib fills. Stage 2: 6-month follow-up for the first launch cohort, after claims run-out.","analysis":"New-user cohort with propensity-score matching or IPTW, Kaplan-Meier persistence curves, Cox models and sensitivity analyses on gap length. Age and site-type interaction tests. Pre-specified stage-wise reporting with no efficacy inference.","instrument":"Claims-based EGFR/line-of-therapy algorithm and an NPI-based site-type classifier, both pre-specified in the SAP","study_design":"Retrospective new-user comparative cohort study in multi-payer US claims (commercial, Medicare Part D/Advantage, Medicaid) with pre-specified staged read-outs and stratified, matched comparison","gap_coverage":"Directly answers the P&T request for claims-based six-month persistence and discontinuation. Adds a matched benchmark, interpretable early read-outs and subgroup strata that the parent does not specify.","cost_effort":"Moderate. Adds a licensed claims extract for the comparator cohorts, staged programming and an SAP. No primary data collection.","timing":"Stage 0 benchmark is available about 3 months after start, before the 2027-03-31 decision. The 6-month Velmara read-out depends on launch timing and claims lag.","feasibility_risks":"Claims cannot confirm EGFR status, so there is a misclassification risk. Early Velmara samples will be small, so Stage 1 is descriptive. Claims lag of 2-3 months. Comparator uptake of NX-441 may be thin. Data licence and access are not yet secured.","post_hoc":false,"prospective_enrolment":false,"protocol_amendment":false,"start_date":null,"evidence_available":null},"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: the expansion adds matched comparators, strata and a pre-specified staging plan to the parent claims design, which improves interpretability over a bare single-arm persistence descriptor. It is weaker than linked EHR-claims data on reasons for discontinuation (see the new alternative). Cost and effort: incremental (extra comparator extract plus staged programming), well below a new primary-data study. Time: a comparator-only benchmark is available before the decision date. Feasibility: depends on existing claims licences. Expanding is the better fit because the parent already targets the exact payer question, and a stand-alone claims study would duplicate it.","population":"Adults with advanced EGFR-mutant NSCLC, identified by a claims algorithm, with a first velmaratinib fill after US launch and at least 12 months of prior continuous enrolment. Comparators are matched post-osimertinib second-line starters.","comparator":"Propensity-matched cohorts starting osimertinib re-challenge, NX-441 (once available) or platinum-based chemotherapy after osimertinib","outcomes":"Six-month persistence (primary), 3-month persistence, time to discontinuation, proportion of days covered, switching and dose-modification proxies, by age and site type","data_source":"US multi-payer claims (commercial, Medicare Part D/Advantage, Medicaid) with specialty-pharmacy fills","study_design":"Retrospective new-user comparative cohort study with pre-specified staged read-outs, propensity-score matching or IPTW and stratified analyses by age and site type","duration_months":15,"readout_lag_months":3,"timing_rationale":"The comparator benchmark can be built from historical claims in the first months. Six-month persistence in the first launch cohort needs about 6 months of follow-up after the 2027-H1 launch plus accrual of the cohort. Claims run-out adds about 2-3 months before the final analysis, hence a 3-month lag.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""},
{"id":"GAP-021:proposal:2","gap_id":"GAP-021","name":"Linked oncology EHR-claims study of velmaratinib persistence and reasons for discontinuation","type":"rwe_study","evidence_question":"What proportion of US patients starting velmaratinib remain on therapy at 6 months, and what clinical reasons (toxicity, progression, access, patient choice) drive discontinuation and dose reduction?","rationale":"Payers want persistence numbers they can trust. Claims show that a patient stopped but not why, and that limits how payers can read the result. A linked EHR-claims dataset adds clinical reasons for stopping, dose changes and treating-site context, which makes the persistence figure interpretable for P&T.","proposal_kind":"new","target_tactic_id":null,"expansion_scope":null,"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: higher clinical validity than claims-only data because reasons for discontinuation and EGFR status come from structured EHR and abstracted notes. Coverage is narrower and less representative than full claims. Cost and effort: higher than expanding TAC-008, because it needs a linked-data licence and chart abstraction. Time: slower, as EHR-linked data lag and the sample is smaller. Feasibility: depends on a data partner with US oncology EHR-claims linkage. A new tactic is justified rather than an expansion because it uses a different data source and answers a different question (why patients stop), so folding it into TAC-008 would change that study's design. It complements TAC-008 and is not a substitute.","population":"Adults with EGFR-mutant advanced NSCLC with a first velmaratinib prescription or fill in US community and academic oncology practices that contribute to the linked EHR-claims dataset","comparator":"None — descriptive, with a historical benchmark from osimertinib-treated patients in the same dataset","outcomes":"Six-month persistence; time to discontinuation; documented reasons for discontinuation (adverse event incl. ILD/QT, progression, cost or access, patient choice); dose reductions and interruptions; site type","data_source":"US oncology EHR linked to claims, with structured data and abstracted physician notes","study_design":"Retrospective-prospective new-user cohort in linked EHR-claims data. Reason-for-discontinuation coding by two abstractors on a sample with adjudication. Kaplan-Meier persistence with competing-risk analysis of discontinuation reasons. Stratified by site type and age.","duration_months":18,"readout_lag_months":4,"timing_rationale":"Needs cohort accrual after the 2027-H1 launch, 6 months of follow-up, and EHR-claims linkage refresh cycles that lag by about 3 months. Chart abstraction and adjudication of discontinuation reasons add about 4 months before a usable readout.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""},
{"id":"GAP-023:proposal:1","gap_id":"GAP-023","name":"External-control comparison of elderly velmaratinib patients versus EU5 and US real-world SoC cohorts","type":"rwe_study","evidence_question":"How do progression-free survival, overall survival and treatment discontinuation in velmaratinib-treated patients aged 65 and over compare with regional standard of care (single-agent chemotherapy or best supportive care) after adjustment for baseline prognostic factors, in EU5 and the US?","rationale":"HTA bodies and US payers said the VEL-301 elderly subgroup is descriptive and that no adjusted comparison against regional standard of care exists. Patient-level velmaratinib data can be compared with real-world SoC cohorts after adjusting for baseline prognosis. This is the closest feasible approach to a comparative answer without a new randomised trial.","proposal_kind":"expansion","target_tactic_id":"TAC-007","expansion_scope":{"name":"External-control comparison of elderly velmaratinib patients versus EU5 and US real-world SoC cohorts","evidence_question":"How do progression-free survival, overall survival and treatment discontinuation in velmaratinib-treated patients aged 65 and over compare with regional standard of care (single-agent chemotherapy or best supportive care) after adjustment for baseline prognostic factors, in EU5 and the US?","population":"Patients aged 65 and over with EGFR-mutant advanced NSCLC after osimertinib. Treated group: velmaratinib-treated patients from the existing VEL-301 data cut and the completed VEL-201 Phase II. Control group: real-world patients on single-agent chemotherapy or best supportive care in EU5 national or hospital data and a US oncology EHR database.","outcomes":"Primary: real-world PFS or time to next treatment/death as a PFS proxy, and overall survival. Secondary: time to treatment discontinuation, and grade 3 or higher safety events where recorded. Reported by region and by age band (65-74, 75+).","geography":"EU5 and US","data_cut":"Existing VEL-301 data cut and the completed VEL-201 dataset. Real-world SoC cohorts are extracted from historical data.","analysis":"Patient-level external-control analysis with IPTW or matching-adjusted weighting on pre-specified prognostic factors (ECOG, brain metastases, line of therapy, comorbidity). Cox models, E-values and quantitative bias analysis. Region-specific estimates with pooled estimates only if heterogeneity is acceptable.","instrument":"Pre-specified target-trial emulation protocol and outcome-alignment rules between trial RECIST PFS and real-world PFS proxies","study_design":"Retrospective external-control comparative cohort study emulating a target trial, combining existing patient-level trial data with real-world SoC cohorts","gap_coverage":"Adds the adjusted velmaratinib-versus-SoC comparison in patients aged 65 and over that stakeholders said does not exist, beyond the descriptive VEL-301 elderly subgroup (TAC-002) and the single-arm EU5 chart review (TAC-003).","cost_effort":"Moderate to high. Needs licensed EU5 and US real-world datasets, statistical programming and an SAP. No new patient enrolment.","timing":"Data-access and curation phase of about 4 months, then analysis. The result will probably follow the 15 January 2027 elderly HTA package and serve later dossier updates, so a faster interim bridge is needed.","feasibility_risks":"Small elderly sample in the trial data, so precision may be limited. Outcome-definition mismatch (RECIST vs real-world PFS). Residual confounding and between-country data heterogeneity. Access to patient-level trial data and sponsor data-sharing approvals are not yet in place.","post_hoc":true,"prospective_enrolment":false,"protocol_amendment":false,"start_date":null,"evidence_available":null},"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: patient-level adjustment against real-world SoC allows covariate balance and sensitivity analysis. It is better than a MAIC on aggregate published data and better than the descriptive subgroup, but still non-randomised. Cost and effort: moderate-to-high due to data licences and programming, far below a new trial. Time: later than a MAIC, so it likely misses the 15 January 2027 package and addresses the 2027-03-31 and later EU5 decisions. Feasibility: depends on trial data access and EU5 data licences. It is posed as an expansion of TAC-007 because that tactic already holds the comparative-effectiveness question in elderly patients, and this adds the design. A new prospective elderly cohort was rejected as too slow for the decision window.","population":"Patients aged 65 and over with EGFR-mutant advanced NSCLC after osimertinib; velmaratinib patients from VEL-301 and VEL-201 data and real-world SoC patients (single-agent chemotherapy or best supportive care) from EU5 and US data","comparator":"Regional real-world standard of care (single-agent chemotherapy or best supportive care) in patients aged 65 and over","outcomes":"Real-world PFS/time to next treatment, overall survival, time to discontinuation, grade 3+ safety where recorded; by region and age band","data_source":"Existing VEL-301 and VEL-201 patient-level data; EU5 national or hospital real-world databases; US oncology EHR database","study_design":"Retrospective external-control comparative cohort study (target-trial emulation) with IPTW or matching-adjusted weighting and quantitative bias analysis","duration_months":6,"readout_lag_months":3,"timing_rationale":"Uses existing data only. Data access, curation and harmonisation take about 4-6 months, mainly EU5 data licensing and the outcome-alignment work. Weighted analysis, bias analyses and report take about 3 months.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""},
{"id":"GAP-023:proposal:2","gap_id":"GAP-023","name":"Anchored and unanchored MAIC of velmaratinib versus published SoC in patients aged 65 and over","type":"maic","evidence_question":"After population adjustment, how do velmaratinib outcomes in patients aged 65 and over compare with published outcomes for single-agent chemotherapy or best supportive care in post-osimertinib EGFR-mutant NSCLC?","rationale":"A MAIC can be produced quickly from existing patient-level VEL-301/VEL-201 data and published aggregate data. It gives HTA reviewers an adjusted comparison for the January 2027 elderly package while the heavier external-control study runs.","proposal_kind":"new","target_tactic_id":null,"expansion_scope":null,"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: lower than the external-control cohort. It relies on published aggregate data, can adjust only for reported effect modifiers and is unanchored where there is no common comparator, so HTA bodies will treat it as supportive. Cost and effort: low (literature targeting plus modelling, no data licences). Time: fastest option and the only one likely to reach the 15 January 2027 package. Feasibility: depends on enough published elderly subgroup data for SoC arms, which is a real risk in the post-osimertinib setting. It is a new tactic rather than an expansion because it is a different method and data source from TAC-007 and does not change that study's design. It serves as an interim bridge alongside, not instead of, the external-control comparison.","population":"Patients aged 65 and over with EGFR-mutant advanced NSCLC after osimertinib; VEL-301 and VEL-201 elderly patients (patient-level data) versus published elderly or age-subgroup SoC arms (aggregate data)","comparator":"Published single-agent or platinum chemotherapy and best supportive care arms reporting outcomes in patients aged 65 and over, identified by a targeted literature search","outcomes":"PFS and overall survival hazard ratios or restricted mean survival time differences, objective response rate, and grade 3+ adverse events where reported","data_source":"Existing VEL-301 and VEL-201 patient-level data; published aggregate trial and observational data identified by a targeted literature search","study_design":"Population-adjusted indirect comparison: MAIC with propensity-score weighting on reported effect modifiers (age, ECOG, brain metastases, prior lines), anchored where a common comparator exists and unanchored otherwise, with sensitivity analyses on the effect-modifier set and effective sample size","duration_months":3,"readout_lag_months":2,"timing_rationale":"Targeted literature search and extraction take about 6-8 weeks. Weighting, analysis and sensitivity runs take about 4-6 weeks using data already held. Internal QC, SAP sign-off and report take about 2 months. Feasibility of meeting 15 January 2027 depends on a start now and on sufficient published data.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""}
