{"tactics":[
{"id":"GAP-001:proposal:1","gap_id":"GAP-001","name":"Procedure-level US claims costing and EU5 tariff mapping within the recurrence HCRU study","type":"hcru_study","evidence_question":"What are the procedure-level direct costs of recurrence after third-generation EGFR TKI progression, from US claims costing and EU5 national tariff mapping, for use as payer-perspective cost-effectiveness model inputs?","rationale":"TAC-006 states the need for procedure-level costs and HCRU after recurrence but does not specify the costing data source, the unit-cost method or the EU5 tariff mapping. This expansion adds those specifications so the outputs can go directly into the CEA (TAC-004) as payer-perspective inputs.","proposal_kind":"expansion","target_tactic_id":"TAC-006","expansion_scope":{"name":"Procedure-level US claims costing and EU5 tariff mapping within the recurrence HCRU study","evidence_question":"What are the procedure-level direct costs of recurrence after third-generation EGFR TKI progression, from US claims costing and EU5 national tariff mapping, for use as payer-perspective cost-effectiveness model inputs?","population":"Adults (18 years or older) with EGFR-mutant advanced NSCLC and a documented recurrence or progression after third-generation EGFR TKI (osimertinib) therapy, with at least 12 months of continuous enrolment or follow-up after the recurrence date","outcomes":"Per-patient and per-month direct cost by procedure category (re-biopsy and liquid biopsy, molecular testing, imaging, radiotherapy, systemic therapy administration, surgery, inpatient stays, ED visits, outpatient visits, supportive care); counts of hospitalisations and ED visits; unit costs mapped to EU5 tariffs (payer perspective, local currency and EUR)","geography":"US (claims) and EU5 (tariff mapping of resource use)","data_cut":"US claims and linked oncology EHR data covering 2019 to the latest available quarter; EU5 national tariff schedules for the latest published year","analysis":"Costs are built bottom-up at the procedure level. US costs use allowed amounts by CPT/HCPCS/ICD-10-PCS code, inflated to a single price year. Per-month costs are estimated with gamma GLMs adjusted for age, comorbidity and CNS involvement. EU5 resource-use frequencies are valued with national tariffs (NHS reference costs, German DRG/EBM, French GHM, Italian DRG, Spanish regional tariffs). Costs are reported with bootstrap intervals and mapped to CEA model health states.","instrument":"","study_design":"Retrospective, descriptive cohort anchored on the recurrence date with 12 months of follow-up. Costing is procedure-level and has no comparator. A standardised procedure-to-tariff crosswalk is built and validated by country health-economics reviewers.","gap_coverage":"Closes the gap directly by supplying procedure-level routine-practice recurrence costs in a form usable by the payer-perspective CEA model.","cost_effort":"Moderate. Needs claims licensing or access, programming and tariff mapping. It extends the planned HCRU study rather than starting a new one.","timing":"About 6 months from data access to final data cut, plus about 3 months to readout. An interim US cost table could be supplied before the 30 Nov 2026 CEA input freeze if data access is secured early.","feasibility_risks":"Linking EGFR status and osimertinib exposure in claims may be incomplete. Claims allowed amounts may not reflect net costs. EU5 tariffs are not actual costs. Claims data access is not yet confirmed.","post_hoc":"","prospective_enrolment":"","protocol_amendment":"","start_date":null,"evidence_available":null},"comparative_rationale":"Compared with a new tactic: a stand-alone costing study would duplicate TAC-006's HCRU and cost question and its cohort build, so expanding is more efficient. Evidence quality is high for the US (actual allowed amounts at procedure level) and moderate for EU5 (tariff proxies). Incremental cost is moderate. Time is about 9 months to readout, which is tight against the 30 Nov 2026 freeze. Feasibility is good, with claims access the main dependency. A literature-based new SLR tactic is faster and cheaper but lower quality and not routine-practice specific, so it is kept as the fallback alternative.","population":"Adults (18 years or older) with EGFR-mutant advanced NSCLC and a documented recurrence or progression after third-generation EGFR TKI (osimertinib) therapy, with at least 12 months of continuous enrolment or follow-up after the recurrence date","comparator":"None — descriptive costing; no comparator","outcomes":"Per-patient and per-month direct cost by procedure category (re-biopsy and liquid biopsy, molecular testing, imaging, radiotherapy, systemic therapy administration, surgery, inpatient stays, ED visits, outpatient visits, supportive care); counts of hospitalisations and ED visits; unit costs mapped to EU5 tariffs (payer perspective, local currency and EUR)","data_source":"US administrative claims linked to oncology EHR where available; EU5 national tariff schedules","study_design":"Retrospective, descriptive cohort anchored on the recurrence date with 12 months of follow-up. Costing is procedure-level and has no comparator. A standardised procedure-to-tariff crosswalk is built and validated by country health-economics reviewers.","duration_months":6,"readout_lag_months":3,"timing_rationale":"Retrospective data already exist, so 6 months covers data access, cohort build and 12-month follow-up cuts. Costing, tariff mapping and model-input QC take about 3 further months.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""},
{"id":"GAP-001:proposal:2","gap_id":"GAP-001","name":"Targeted SLR and tariff-based unit costing of recurrence procedures after osimertinib","type":"slr","evidence_question":"What published and tariff-based unit costs and resource-use frequencies apply to procedures used after recurrence on third-generation EGFR TKI in EGFR-mutant NSCLC, for the payer-perspective CEA?","rationale":"The library has no SLR on recurrence costs. A rapid, structured synthesis of published costs plus public tariffs can supply provisional model inputs and a validation benchmark for the claims-based costing, and it protects the CEA input freeze if claims access is delayed.","proposal_kind":"new","target_tactic_id":null,"expansion_scope":null,"comparative_rationale":"Versus expanding TAC-006: the SLR is faster (about 4 months total) and cheaper, and needs no data access. But its evidence quality is lower. It relies on heterogeneous published sources, mixed years and settings, and may include few studies on post-osimertinib populations, so it is not routine-practice costing from a defined cohort. Feasibility is high. It is best used as a fallback or complement. It does not duplicate any library tactic, since TAC-006 is a primary-data HCRU study and TAC-004 is a model.","population":"Adults with EGFR-mutant advanced NSCLC progressing after third-generation EGFR TKI (osimertinib) or any EGFR TKI; procedures include re-biopsy, liquid biopsy, imaging, radiotherapy, chemotherapy administration, hospitalisation and ED visits","comparator":"None — descriptive synthesis of unit costs and resource use","outcomes":"Unit cost per procedure and per hospital or ED episode (price-year adjusted); resource-use frequencies after recurrence; study-quality assessment (CHEERS-based); tariff-based cost tables for US, UK, DE, FR, IT and ES","data_source":"MEDLINE, Embase, EconLit, NHS EED/CRD, ISPOR and ASCO abstracts; CMS fee schedules; EU5 national tariff schedules (NHS reference costs, G-DRG/EBM, GHM, Italian DRG, Spanish regional tariffs)","study_design":"Targeted SLR following PRISMA with a pre-specified protocol, dual screening and data extraction, and CHEERS-based quality appraisal. Narrative synthesis, plus a tariff-based unit-cost table for payer-perspective inputs.","duration_months":3,"readout_lag_months":1,"timing_rationale":"A targeted SLR with dual screening takes about 3 months. The tariff table and a CEA-ready input report need about 1 more month.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""},
{"id":"GAP-020:proposal:1","gap_id":"GAP-020","name":"Bayesian borrowing and precision analysis for pooled intracranial response and duration","type":"secondary_analysis","evidence_question":"How precise are intracranial ORR and duration of intracranial response when the VEL-301 CNS sub-study is combined with VEL-201 Phase II measurable-brain-metastasis patients using Bayesian dynamic borrowing, and do they support a durability claim?","rationale":"TAC-024 plans a pooled CNS analysis with VEL-201 but does not specify a borrowing method, precision targets or prior-data conflict assessment. This expansion adds those specifications so that precision can be quantified for payers and HTA bodies.","proposal_kind":"expansion","target_tactic_id":"TAC-024","expansion_scope":{"name":"Bayesian borrowing and precision analysis for pooled intracranial response and duration","evidence_question":"How precise are intracranial ORR and duration of intracranial response when the VEL-301 CNS sub-study is combined with VEL-201 Phase II measurable-brain-metastasis patients using Bayesian dynamic borrowing, and do they support a durability claim?","population":"Patients with measurable brain metastases at baseline: the VEL-301 CNS sub-study cohort (about 140 patients planned) and the 29 VEL-201 Phase II patients with measurable brain metastases after osimertinib","outcomes":"Intracranial ORR and duration of intracranial response (median, landmark rates at 6 and 12 months) with posterior credible intervals; effective sample size contributed by VEL-201; prior-data conflict diagnostics; probability that the lower credible bound exceeds pre-specified payer-relevant thresholds","geography":"US + EU5 (trial sites)","data_cut":"VEL-201 final dataset; VEL-301 CNS sub-study first protocol-specified data cut, BICR-assessed intracranial response","analysis":"Robust meta-analytic-predictive or mixture-prior Bayesian dynamic borrowing from VEL-201 into VEL-301 for ORR (binomial) and for duration of response (piecewise exponential or Kaplan-Meier-informed parametric). Pre-specified precision metrics (credible interval width, effective sample size). Sensitivity analyses: no borrowing, discounted borrowing, and a skeptical prior. Simulation of expected precision under pessimistic scenarios.","instrument":"","study_design":"Pre-specified statistical analysis plan (SAP) for a secondary analysis pooling VEL-201 and VEL-301 CNS sub-study patient-level data. Single-arm intracranial estimation with harmonised eligibility and response criteria; no new patient enrolment.","gap_coverage":"Quantifies whether the CNS sub-study alone and the pooled data yield adequately precise intracranial response and duration estimates, and states the assumptions behind a durability claim.","cost_effort":"Low to moderate. Biostatistics effort, dataset harmonisation and SAP, with no new data collection.","timing":"About 4 months of analysis once CNS sub-study data are available, plus about 2 months to readout. The CNS sub-study timeline itself determines whether this meets the 2027-03-31 decision.","feasibility_risks":"VEL-201 is single-arm Phase II with an older population mix, so prior-data conflict may limit borrowing. Regulators and HTA bodies may question borrowed priors. Patient-level data access and harmonised response assessment are not yet confirmed.","post_hoc":"Yes — analysis of the completed VEL-201 dataset and of VEL-301 CNS data using existing data only","prospective_enrolment":"","protocol_amendment":"","start_date":null,"evidence_available":null},"comparator":"None — single-arm intracranial estimation; no between-arm comparison","outcomes":"Intracranial ORR and duration of intracranial response (median, landmark rates at 6 and 12 months) with posterior credible intervals; effective sample size contributed by VEL-201; prior-data conflict diagnostics; probability that the lower credible bound exceeds pre-specified payer-relevant thresholds","data_source":"VEL-201 Phase II patient-level data and VEL-301 CNS sub-study data","population":"Patients with measurable brain metastases at baseline: the VEL-301 CNS sub-study cohort (about 140 patients planned) and the 29 VEL-201 Phase II patients with measurable brain metastases after osimertinib","study_design":"Pre-specified statistical analysis plan (SAP) for a secondary analysis pooling VEL-201 and VEL-301 CNS sub-study patient-level data. Single-arm intracranial estimation with harmonised eligibility and response criteria; no new patient enrolment.","duration_months":4,"readout_lag_months":2,"timing_rationale":"Analysis uses existing data. About 4 months covers dataset harmonisation, SAP and borrowing/sensitivity runs. About 2 months more is needed for QC, reporting and documentation for payer and HTA dossiers.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":"","comparative_rationale":"Compared with the alternative expansion of TAC-010 (enriched enrolment and extended follow-up): Bayesian borrowing is much faster and cheaper and needs no protocol amendment, so it is the only option likely to read out before the 2027-03-31 decision. Its evidence quality is moderate. It adds only 29 external patients, depends on prior assumptions and does not increase actual sample size. Enrichment gives stronger, assumption-free precision but costs much more and cannot read out in time. A new stand-alone tactic would duplicate TAC-024's pooled-CNS scope, so expanding is preferred."},
{"id":"GAP-020:proposal:2","gap_id":"GAP-020","name":"Enriched enrolment and extended intracranial follow-up in the VEL-301 CNS sub-study","type":"subgroup_analysis","evidence_question":"Does adding patients with measurable brain metastases and extending intracranial follow-up in the VEL-301 CNS sub-study give intracranial ORR and duration of response estimates precise enough to support a durability claim?","rationale":"The roughly 140-patient CNS sub-study is the main source of intracranial evidence, and its size and duration-of-response follow-up are the concern. Adding patients and extending follow-up addresses the sample and maturity limits directly.","proposal_kind":"expansion","target_tactic_id":"TAC-010","expansion_scope":{"name":"Enriched enrolment and extended intracranial follow-up in the VEL-301 CNS sub-study","evidence_question":"Does adding patients with measurable brain metastases and extending intracranial follow-up in the VEL-301 CNS sub-study give intracranial ORR and duration of response estimates precise enough to support a durability claim?","population":"Patients in VEL-301 with measurable brain metastases at baseline (RECIST 1.1/RANO-BM), with additional enrichment of about 60 patients over the planned approximately 140","outcomes":"BICR-assessed intracranial ORR and duration of intracranial response (median and landmark rates at 6, 12 and 18 months), time to intracranial progression, CNS progression-free survival, radiotherapy for new brain lesions by arm, precision (95% CI width) of ORR and median DoR","geography":"US + EU5 VEL-301 sites","data_cut":"Primary CNS sub-study data cut plus a second extended cut with at least 12 months of follow-up after the first intracranial response in all responders","analysis":"Pre-specified precision targets (for example CI half-width of about 8 percentage points for ORR). Kaplan-Meier DoR with competing-risk handling of death and non-CNS progression. Exploratory descriptive comparison with the control arm where measurable-BM patients exist.","instrument":"","study_design":"Enriched enrolment of measurable-brain-metastasis patients by site targeting and a screening-eligibility flag, with extended protocol-specified intracranial imaging (every 8 weeks for 12 months, then every 12 weeks) and blinded independent central review under the existing VEL-301 protocol.","gap_coverage":"Directly increases sample size and the maturity of duration data, reducing reliance on assumptions for the durability claim.","cost_effort":"High. Additional enrolment, MRI imaging, BICR reads and site costs, plus the regulatory workload for an amendment.","timing":"The amendment, site activation, enrolment and 12-month follow-up take about 18 months. The extended cut is unlikely before the 2027-03-31 decision and would mainly support later HTA updates and re-submissions.","feasibility_risks":"Needs a credible protocol amendment on VEL-301 (an active study), which is not approved. Competing studies in measurable-BM patients may slow enrolment. Added imaging burden may reduce adherence. Interim looks risk operational and statistical-integrity problems.","post_hoc":"","prospective_enrolment":"About 60 additional patients with measurable brain metastases","protocol_amendment":"Required: protocol amendment to VEL-301 to enrich enrolment and extend intracranial imaging and follow-up. Not yet submitted or approved.","start_date":null,"evidence_available":null},"comparator":"None — single-arm intracranial estimation; control-arm comparison exploratory where measurable-BM patients exist","outcomes":"BICR-assessed intracranial ORR and duration of intracranial response (median and landmark rates at 6, 12 and 18 months), time to intracranial progression, CNS progression-free survival, radiotherapy for new brain lesions by arm, precision (95% CI width) of ORR and median DoR","data_source":"Prospective VEL-301 CNS sub-study clinical trial data (MRI with BICR)","population":"Patients in VEL-301 with measurable brain metastases at baseline (RECIST 1.1/RANO-BM), with additional enrichment of about 60 patients over the planned approximately 140","study_design":"Enriched enrolment of measurable-brain-metastasis patients by site targeting and a screening-eligibility flag, with extended protocol-specified intracranial imaging (every 8 weeks for 12 months, then every 12 weeks) and blinded independent central review under the existing VEL-301 protocol.","duration_months":18,"readout_lag_months":3,"timing_rationale":"About 3 months for amendment and site activation, 6 months of added enrolment, and at least 12 months of response follow-up overlapping the enrolment, giving about 18 months to last data. About 3 months for BICR reconciliation, database lock and analysis.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":"","comparative_rationale":"Compared with Bayesian borrowing (expansion of TAC-024): enrichment and extension gives the highest evidence quality because it adds real patients and mature duration data. It is also the most costly and slowest (about 21 months), and needs an amendment on an active study, so it would not read out by 2027-03-31. Borrowing is cheaper and quicker but rests on prior assumptions. A separate new study would duplicate TAC-010's sub-study infrastructure and TAC-011's real-world CNS scope, so expanding TAC-010 is more efficient and keeps the control-arm context."}
]}
