{"tactics":[
{"id":"GAP-001:proposal:1","gap_id":"GAP-001","name":"TAC-006 expansion: US claims procedure-level cost of recurrence after third-generation EGFR TKI","type":"hcru_study","evidence_question":"What are the payer-paid direct costs, by procedure category, and associated healthcare resource use in the 12 months after recurrence on a third-generation EGFR TKI in US routine care?","rationale":"The CEA versus osimertinib needs observed, procedure-level US payer costs of recurrence. TAC-006 names this question but has no population, data source or design. This expansion makes it a runnable retrospective claims analysis. It is timed so that an interim cost table is ready before the 30 November 2026 CEA and budget-impact input freeze and the final table follows shortly after. It is the primary routine-practice answer to the gap. The micro-costing proposal (GAP-001:proposal:2) is its fallback and validation benchmark, and covers EU5.","proposal_kind":"expansion","target_tactic_id":"TAC-006","expansion_scope":{"name":"TAC-006 expansion: US claims procedure-level cost of recurrence after third-generation EGFR TKI","evidence_question":"What are the payer-paid direct costs, by procedure category, and associated healthcare resource use in the 12 months after recurrence on a third-generation EGFR TKI in US routine care?","population":"Adults (18+) with advanced or metastatic (stage IV or recurrent) NSCLC and at least one osimertinib dispensing of 60 or more days between 2019 and 2025, with a claims-based recurrence/progression event. Continuous enrolment of at least 6 months before osimertinib start. Patients with claims-evident adjuvant osimertinib use (resection within 12 months before the first dispensing and no stage IV coding) are excluded. Pre-specified strata: age 65+ versus under 65, CNS-directed versus non-CNS recurrence, and EGFR-testing-confirmed versus not confirmed.","outcomes":"Primary: mean and median per-patient payer-allowed and plan-paid direct costs in the 6 and 12 months after the recurrence index date, split by procedure category (imaging, biopsy and molecular testing, radiotherapy including SRS, systemic therapy drug and administration, surgery, inpatient stays, ED visits, outpatient visits, supportive and palliative care). Secondary: counts and per-patient-per-month rates of hospitalisations, length of stay, ED visits and procedures; share of patients with CNS-directed procedures; costs by age, CNS and EGFR-confirmation stratum.","geography":"US","data_cut":"Latest available quarterly claims refresh, with data complete through at least 2025-12-31. A preliminary extract on the first 60% of the cohort build supports the interim cost table.","analysis":"Primary recurrence algorithm: the index date is the first of (a) a switch to, or addition of, a different systemic anticancer regimen after at least 60 days of osimertinib, or (b) a new SRS/WBRT course while osimertinib continues (CNS-directed recurrence). Stricter sensitivity definitions: S1 requires a claims-documented EGFR test (single-gene or NGS) in the 12 months before osimertinib start; S2 requires (a) plus a restaging imaging claim within 30 days before the index date; S3 excludes radiotherapy-only events. Patients with osimertinib dispensing but no EGFR test claim stay in the primary analysis, because osimertinib use is a proxy for EGFR-mutant disease, and are reported as a separate stratum. If their mean 12-month costs differ from the EGFR-confirmed stratum by more than 10%, the EGFR-confirmed cohort becomes the CEA base case and the full cohort a scenario. Descriptive cost and utilisation estimates with bootstrapped 95% CIs. Gamma-family GLM with log link for adjusted costs. Censoring-adjusted cost estimation for patients who die or disenrol. Costs inflated to a single 2026 USD base year. Outputs formatted as unit costs and event frequencies by CEA health state.","instrument":"","study_design":"Retrospective, descriptive cohort study of already-collected licensed US administrative claims (commercial and Medicare), with no prospective enrolment. The index date is the claims-defined recurrence event. Follow-up is up to 12 months, ending at death, disenrolment or data cut. There is no comparator arm; this is descriptive costing for CEA input. Planned start is 2026-07-06. An interim cost table by procedure category is due by 2026-10-30, with the final report in early November 2026. If data access is not executed by 2026-08-14, the CEA freeze uses the micro-costing inputs (GAP-001:proposal:2) and the claims results are added as an update before the 2027-03-31 decision date.","gap_coverage":"Directly fills the gap: observed US payer procedure-level direct costs of recurrence for the CEA. This is routine-practice evidence, not modelled or fee-schedule estimates. It covers the US only. EU5 costs are not covered and rely on the tariff-based micro-costing proposal (GAP-001:proposal:2) or separate country sources.","cost_effort":"Moderate. Needs a claims data licence, a protocol and SAP, and biostatistics and epidemiology time. No patient recruitment or site costs. Lower cost than a prospective or linked-EHR design.","timing":"About 3 months from start to final data cut (build, interim extract, final extract) and about 1 month to final readout. Planned start is 2026-07-06, last data in about early October 2026, and final readout about early November 2026. An interim table is due 2026-10-30, ahead of the 30 November 2026 freeze.","feasibility_risks":"This is a post-hoc analysis of already-collected claims data: no prospective enrolment and no protocol amendment. Claims lack EGFR mutation status and progression dates, so both are proxied by osimertinib use and treatment-change algorithms. Misclassification is addressed by sensitivity definitions S1-S3 and the EGFR-confirmation stratum. CNS procedures may be under-captured. Claims show payer-allowed amounts, not full societal cost. Data licence and access are not yet secured and nothing here is approved; the 2026-08-14 access checkpoint triggers the fallback described in study_design. EU5 costs are out of scope.","post_hoc":true,"prospective_enrolment":false,"protocol_amendment":false,"start_date":"2026-07-06","evidence_available":null},"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: the claims expansion gives observed routine-practice, procedure-level US payer costs, which is the evidence type payers expect. A fee-schedule micro-costing plus SLR is only modelled, so its quality is lower. Cost and effort: moderate, building on TAC-006 without a duplicate study; micro-costing is cheaper but weaker, and a prospective HCRU or linked-EHR study would be too slow and costly. Time: about 4 months from the planned start to final readout, with an interim table before the freeze. Feasibility: depends on timely claims access and recurrence-proxy validity. Both are mitigated by the 2026-08-14 checkpoint with micro-costing fallback, and by the sensitivity analyses.","population":"Adults (18+) with advanced or metastatic (stage IV or recurrent) NSCLC and at least one osimertinib dispensing of 60 or more days between 2019 and 2025, with a claims-based recurrence/progression event. Continuous enrolment of at least 6 months before osimertinib start. Patients with claims-evident adjuvant osimertinib use (resection within 12 months before the first dispensing and no stage IV coding) are excluded. Pre-specified strata: age 65+ versus under 65, CNS-directed versus non-CNS recurrence, and EGFR-testing-confirmed versus not confirmed.","comparator":"None — descriptive cost analysis; strata compared are age 65+ versus under 65, CNS versus non-CNS recurrence, and EGFR-confirmed versus not confirmed","outcomes":"Primary: mean and median per-patient payer-allowed and plan-paid direct costs in the 6 and 12 months after the recurrence index date, split by procedure category (imaging, biopsy and molecular testing, radiotherapy including SRS, systemic therapy drug and administration, surgery, inpatient stays, ED visits, outpatient visits, supportive and palliative care). Secondary: counts and per-patient-per-month rates of hospitalisations, length of stay, ED visits and procedures; share of patients with CNS-directed procedures; costs by age, CNS and EGFR-confirmation stratum.","data_source":"Licensed US administrative claims database (commercial and Medicare), subject to data access","study_design":"Retrospective, descriptive cohort study of already-collected licensed US administrative claims (commercial and Medicare), with no prospective enrolment. The index date is the claims-defined recurrence event. Follow-up is up to 12 months, ending at death, disenrolment or data cut. There is no comparator arm; this is descriptive costing for CEA input. Planned start is 2026-07-06. An interim cost table by procedure category is due by 2026-10-30, with the final report in early November 2026. If data access is not executed by 2026-08-14, the CEA freeze uses the micro-costing inputs (GAP-001:proposal:2) and the claims results are added as an update before the 2027-03-31 decision date.","duration_months":3,"readout_lag_months":1,"timing_rationale":"The data already exist, so there is no enrolment. About 3 months covers protocol and SAP, cohort build, an interim extract and the final data cut, starting 2026-07-06. About 1 month covers cost modelling, QC and a CEA-ready report, with an interim table by 2026-10-30. Starting in early July leaves a margin before the 30 November 2026 freeze. A missed access checkpoint triggers the fallback.","withdraw":false,"withdraw_reason":""},
{"id":"GAP-001:proposal:2","gap_id":"GAP-001","name":"Pre-registered targeted SLR and micro-costing of recurrence procedures after third-generation EGFR TKI (fallback and benchmark for claims analysis)","type":"slr","evidence_question":"What are the unit costs and expected frequencies of the procedures used to manage recurrence after third-generation EGFR TKI progression from a US payer perspective (Medicare and commercial) and in EU5, based on published pathways, public price files and graded clinician validation?","rationale":"This is a fallback and validation benchmark for the claims-based recurrence cost study in TAC-006, not a stand-alone answer to the routine-practice gap. It supplies a transparent procedure-level cost input if claims access slips past the 30 November 2026 freeze. It also provides a cross-check for the claims results and is the source of EU5 tariff inputs. It does not duplicate TAC-006 because it uses different data and methods and no patient-level claims.","proposal_kind":"new","target_tactic_id":null,"expansion_scope":null,"comparative_rationale":"Evidence quality: modelled micro-costing from literature and price files is lower quality than observed claims costs (the TAC-006 expansion), because it does not reflect actual routine-practice utilisation. Per-input evidence grades and commercial-payer scenarios make that limitation visible and partly offset it. Cost and effort: the lowest option, using desk research and a small panel with no data licence. Time: about 4 months including the readout, so it can deliver before the 30 November 2026 freeze with margin. Feasibility: high, because it uses public sources, but published procedure-frequency data after osimertinib are sparse, so many inputs will rely on lower-grade evidence. Positioned as a fallback, benchmark and EU5 input source rather than a replacement for the claims study.","population":"Adults with advanced EGFR-mutant NSCLC progressing on a third-generation EGFR TKI (osimertinib), including CNS and non-CNS recurrence, as described in published studies and clinical pathways. US payer perspective covering Medicare and commercial payers, with EU5 national tariffs as a secondary extraction.","comparator":"None — descriptive costing and evidence synthesis","outcomes":"Unit cost per recurrence-related procedure (imaging, biopsy and molecular re-testing, radiotherapy including SRS, drug administration, surgery, hospital admission, ED visit, supportive care) under Medicare and commercial-payer scenarios; published frequency of each procedure per recurrence episode with an evidence grade per input; expected direct cost per recurrence episode with low-high ranges; EU5 tariff-based unit costs; evidence-gap table by procedure; percentage difference from the claims-based per-episode cost once available.","data_source":"PubMed/Embase and ISPOR/ASCO/WCLC abstract searches (last 10 years); CMS Physician Fee Schedule, OPPS, IPPS/MS-DRG and ASP pricing files; published commercial-to-Medicare price ratios and commercial cost sources; EU5 national tariff schedules; structured two-round Delphi panel of about 6-8 US oncologists and payer/pharmacy experts","study_design":"Protocol pre-registered (for example on OSF) before searching. Targeted systematic literature review limited to cost-of-illness and resource-use studies after third-generation EGFR TKI progression, with dual-reviewer screening of titles/abstracts and full texts and single extraction with second-reviewer verification. Bottom-up micro-costing: map procedures to CPT/HCPCS/DRG codes and apply CMS fee-schedule rates. Drugs are costed at ASP-based rates. Commercial-payer costs are modelled as scenarios using published commercial-to-Medicare multipliers (hospital and physician services) and commercial ASP markups, with ranges. Each frequency input receives an evidence grade: A observed US claims or registry data, B published observational or non-US data, C Delphi-only, D analyst assumption. Literature values take precedence over Delphi values. Delphi results are reported as medians with IQR and an agreement threshold (70% within a pre-set range), with equal expert weighting. Unresolved inputs are flagged as low-confidence and widened in probabilistic sensitivity ranges for the CEA. When TAC-006 claims results are available, per-episode costs are compared and differences above 25% are investigated.","duration_months":3,"readout_lag_months":1,"timing_rationale":"The search is narrowed to cost and resource-use studies over 10 years and pre-registered, with dual screening plus extraction taking about 2 months. In parallel, fee-schedule mapping and the first Delphi round run in the same period, and a second Delphi round and EU5 tariff extraction extend into month 3. About 1 month then covers synthesis, grading, costing and a CEA-ready input table. The total of about 4 months leaves margin before the 30 November 2026 freeze if work starts by early July."
